{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1539196182,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWUzNjg3OTQ3MTU=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2910","origin":"github"},"title":"RFC: our reliance on PWD env var backfires...","message":"#### What is the problem?\nIf users use datalad as a python module, and they, or some other process (jupyter?), switches to another workdir (obviously using `os.chdir`, not our `chpwd`) then any datalad command operating on relative filenames would fail with confusing errors or just would do the wrong thing... causes confusion, frustration, etc. Definitely we should mitigate it somehow.\n\nBackground: Use of env var PWD was introduced with the sole purpose of being able to operate on (compare etc) full paths while some directory in the path is a symlink and then `os.getcwd()` would return dereferenced path. It might be that one of the initial use-cases was a \"subdataset\" which is just symlinked from another location? because otherwise I think we could have just used/report full dereferenced path.  Overall - not sure now what problem we were/are solving besides reporting the full path user expects to see whenever in his shell he is within such a symlinked directory.\n\nAnd now I wonder what should we do in respect to the original problem that we are not really in full control of current directory.  I see possibilities\n\n- in `getpwd` verify that envvar PWD and `os.getpwd()` point to the same directory (after realpath'ing it), and if not - return `os.getpwd()` (with some warning?).\n- completely eliminate reliance on PWD and use `getpwd()`.  That might ruin that use-case which might no longer exist, and would cause a somewhat confusing reported paths not matching what user expects (e.g. on OSX IIRC users' homes might be symlinked etc)","files":null}]}